is message by adding the pragma `{attribute 'suppresswarning' := 'SA0027'}` **to the library manager** / project\". In older versions, `suppresswarning` unknown; maybe there's a different pragma **`{pragma 'suppresswarning' := 'SA0027'}`**? The compiler said \"The attribute suppresswarning is unknown\", meaning it parsed as attribute, but maybe suppression pragmas use **`{pragma ...}`** not `{attribute ...}`? In CODESYS, compiler pragmas are indeed `{attribute ...}`. There is also **`{warning disable 'C0351'}`**? Hmm.\n\nCODESYS uses **`{attribute 'suppresswarning' := 'C0371'}`** in recent versions. If unknown in this 3.5.22 version, perhaps static analysis check supports **`{attribute 'suppresswarning' := 'SA0027'}` only on the *message category* not FB**. It was unknown globally though. Maybe correct is **`{attribute 'suppresswarning' := 'SA0027'}` placed before the variable** and compiler still says attribute unknown? The compiler said unknown at line 1, so no.\n\nCould use **`{attribute 'disable' := 'SA0027'}`**? no.\n\nMaybe additional code checks are **not affected by attributes** but can be disabled via a **special POU name suffix**? no.\n\nLet's look at the project build output: \"Message(s) of additional code checks available. See category 'Additional code checks'\". That is a build step from **CODESYS Static Analysis** integrated. Static analysis messages SA####. There may be a **pragma `{attribute 'analysis' := 'none'}`** but it didn't work, perhaps because static analysis is configured to run on all POUs regardless. Maybe correct value is **`'off'`** rather than `'none'`. Let's research static analysis pragma values from memory:\n- In CODESYS, you can exclude a POU from static analysis by adding the pragma `{attribute 'analysis' := 'none'}`. I'm fairly confident. Why didn't it? Maybe the attribute must be **inside** the FB declaration, not before; though example said before. Or perhaps value is **`'no'`**. Hmm.\n- There is `{attribute 'analysis' := 'hide